{#
  Migrations 0117 / 0118 / 0119 — THE MONEY PATH.

  Authority: ARCHITECTURE.md §6.5 (the gate transaction, statement by statement, and
  the latency budget), §5.5, §5.6, §16; research/05-architecture/merge-gate-invariant.md
  §4-§5 (anomalies A1-A14); spec/errors.md §2, §3.3, §4; docs/leads/kernel.md D4, D6,
  D10, MR-8.

  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ONE SERVER-SIDE PROCEDURE, SO THE TRANSACTION CANNOT BE HALF-ISSUED
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  §6.5's argument is that the client pays one round trip and a client crash cannot
  leave the merge half-performed. That is the whole reason this is a `CREATE
  PROCEDURE … LANGUAGE PLpgSQL` invoked by `CALL` rather than eight statements in
  `trappoint_core`. The client's job shrinks to: set the isolation level explicitly,
  `CALL`, and discriminate the SQLSTATE.

  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  THE PROCEDURE REFUSES ALMOST NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  §6.5's pseudocode tests the state and the counters and returns GATE_REFUSED. Taken
  literally that produces a refusal with NO CONSTRAINT NAME, and the constraint name
  is the exhibit. Every one of those conditions already has a named mechanism that
  refuses it, and the conformance manifest names each one:

    state is not the completing state → 23503 on the event table's `legal_edge`
                                        composite FK into `subject_transition` (CF-13)
    an obligation counter is non-zero → 23514 on that counter's own CHECK (CF-01,
                                        CF-05, CF-31, CF-52)
    no merged_commit                  → 23514 on `merge_evidence` (CF-04)
    the head moved                    → 23505 on the event table's `linear` CAS (CF-14)
    already merged                    → 23505 on `merge_record_pkey` (CF-09)
    an obligation arrived after issue → 23503 on the epoch pin (CF-10, MI07)
    drift, expiry, absent authority   → P0001 from `fn_<subject>_merge_gate`
                                        (CF-02, CF-03, CF-06, CF-53)

  So the procedure sets up those mechanisms and gets out of the way. It raises `P0001`
  in exactly two places, and both are conditions no other mechanism can hold:

    * the subject does not exist — absence of a row, `spec/errors.md` §2.5 case 2;
    * the head moved between the anchor read and the final `UPDATE` — the compare-and
      -swap in the last write matched zero rows, and a procedure that returned
      successfully having written a `merge_record` for a merge that did not happen
      would be the single worst defect in this repository.

  It also does NOT pre-empt `fn_<subject>_merge_gate`. Conformance cases CF-02, CF-03
  and CF-06 name that function as the exhibit; a duplicate re-derivation here that
  raised first would produce the right SQLSTATE with the wrong object attached, and
  the object name is the whole diagnosis for `P0001`.

  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  STATEMENT ORDER IS AN ARGUMENT ABOUT DEFERRABILITY
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  `DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED` is unimplemented on CockroachDB, so every
  intermediate state must be legal at a statement boundary and the subject's own six
  CHECKs must fire on the LAST write. Hence: event, then completion record, then
  ledger intake, then the `UPDATE` that moves the state. The completion record takes
  its composite foreign key onto `({{ subjects[0].id_column }}, {{ subjects[0].epoch_column }})` while the subject is still in
  its pre-completion state, which is legal, and the subsequent `UPDATE` does not
  touch {{ subjects[0].epoch_column }} — measured on v26.2.5: an `UPDATE` of a NON-key column of a row
  referenced under `ON UPDATE RESTRICT` is accepted, and an `UPDATE` of the referenced
  key is refused with `23503` naming the pin. Both halves of that were measured
  because the whole epoch-pin claim rests on the second and the whole gate rests on
  the first.

  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  D6, AND THE ONE PLACE THIS FILE DEPARTS FROM IT
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Ruling D6 puts the kernel procedures in the `trappoint` bootstrap schema, as
  `trappoint.merge_permit()`. These procedures are emitted into the BINDING's own
  schema instead — `{{ binding.schema }}.merge_permit()` — for one reason that D6 could not have
  weighed, because it is created by D6's own neighbour: strategy §1.1 ships a SECOND
  binding, and the reference vertical's own binding file discusses "a cluster carrying
  BOTH bindings". Two bindings rendering `trappoint.merge_permit(UUID, BYTES, …)` is
  one object, and whichever migration ran second would silently redefine the other
  vertical's gate against the wrong tables. Every other object these templates emit is
  already `{{ binding.schema }}`-scoped; the bootstrap schema keeps the three objects that are
  genuinely cluster-global (`schema_migration`, `schema_lock`, `schema_attestation`).
  A cross-domain note is filed with this worker's output.

  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  PLATFORM GROUND TRUTH MEASURED FOR THESE FILES (CockroachDB CCL v26.2.5, local node)
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  * `GET DIAGNOSTICS … = ROW_COUNT` is NOT IMPLEMENTED (`0A000`, cockroachdb#117410).
    The head compare-and-swap therefore uses `UPDATE … RETURNING … INTO`, which leaves
    the target variable NULL when the `WHERE` matched nothing. Measured working.
  * `SELECT count(*) … FOR SHARE` is REFUSED (`0A000: FOR SHARE is not allowed with
    aggregate functions`). §6.5 is written that way. The locking read is a sub-select
    and the aggregate sits outside it — measured working.
  * `SELECT … INTO … FOR UPDATE` inside PL/pgSQL: measured working.
  * `CREATE PROCEDURE` binds EARLY: a reference to a relation that does not exist is
    refused at creation time (`42P01`), measured. So (a) these migrations must run
    after every relation they name, which the 0117-0119 allocation guarantees, and
    (b) ANY later change to a referenced table requires `CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE`
    in the same migration. That is why every procedure here is written as
    `CREATE OR REPLACE`: the replacement path must already be the shipped path.
  * `digest()`, `string_agg(… ORDER BY …)`, `jsonb_build_object`, `||` on JSONB and
    `RAISE … USING ERRCODE=…, MESSAGE=<expression>`: all measured working.
  * Ruling D4 held: no `CASE` anywhere; no loop, no `EXECUTE`, no `PERFORM`.
#}
{% set genesis = "decode(repeat('00', 32), 'hex')" %}
{% for s in subjects %}
{% set stem = s.id_column[:-3] %}
{% set counter = (s.counters | selectattr('column', 'equalto', 'open_blocking') | list
                  or s.counters | selectattr('polarity', 'equalto', 'zero_when_complete') | list)
                 | first %}
-- @file 0{{ 117 + loop.index0 }}_proc_merge_{{ s.kind }}.sql
{{ header(file='0' ~ (117 + loop.index0) ~ '_proc_merge_' ~ s.kind ~ '.sql',
          title='CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.merge_' ~ s.kind ~ '() — the gate transaction as one server-side statement',
          rationale='One round trip, and a client crash cannot leave the transition half-performed. The procedure sets up the named mechanisms and gets out of their way: it refuses only the two conditions nothing else can hold — a subject that does not exist, and a head that moved between the anchor read and the last write — because every other refusal on this path already has a constraint name attached, and the constraint name is the exhibit.',
          mi=['MI09', 'MI24'] if loop.first else ['MI09', 'MI30'],
          i=['I03', 'I04'],
          counsel_gated=false) }}
--
-- SUBJECT: {{ s.kind }} ({{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} → {{ binding.schema }}.{{ stem }}_event → {{ binding.schema }}.merge_record).
--
-- THE PARAMETERS, and why the evidentiary ones are supplied rather than computed.
-- `a_canon_bytes` is RFC 8785 JCS over `a_payload` and `a_leaf_hash` is
-- SHA-256(0x00 || canon_bytes), both produced by the CLIENT. SQL cannot canonicalise
-- to JCS — CockroachDB's own JSONB key ordering is not reproducible by a third party
-- — so a server-computed leaf would be a hash nobody outside this cluster could
-- recompute, which is the opposite of what a custody ledger is for. What the SERVER
-- computes is everything a client could lie about and a reader could check:
-- `clearance_digest`, `prev_digest`, the observed obligation count, and the sequence.
--
-- ISOLATION IS THE CALLER'S. This procedure does not and cannot set it: `SET
-- TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE` is issued by `trappoint_core.gate` on
-- every attempt, explicitly, never inherited from a pool default (spec/errors.md
-- §2.1). A procedure that silently ran at whatever the session offered would make the
-- one line of the client that matters unauditable.

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE {{ binding.schema }}.merge_{{ s.kind }}(
  a_subject_id    UUID,
  a_merged_commit BYTES,
  a_merged_by     STRING,
  a_actor_kind    STRING,
  a_payload       JSONB,
  a_canon_bytes   BYTES,
  a_payload_ver   INT2,
  a_leaf_hash     BYTES
) LANGUAGE PLpgSQL AS $proc$
DECLARE
  v_state       {{ binding.schema }}.subject_state;
  v_head        INT8;
  v_epoch       INT8;
{% if binding.emit_outbox %}
  v_site_id     UUID;
  v_site_code   STRING;
{% endif %}
  v_open        INT8;
  v_prev_digest BYTES;
  v_clearance   BYTES;
  v_new_head    INT8;
BEGIN
  -- ── 1 · ANCHOR THE SUBJECT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- `FOR UPDATE` is LOCK ORDERING AND RETRY-THRASH REDUCTION ONLY, never
  -- correctness. Under SERIALIZABLE these locks are unreplicated and best-effort
  -- unless `enable_durable_locking_for_serializable` is set, and nothing here depends
  -- on them either way: correctness comes from SERIALIZABLE plus the compare-and-swap
  -- in step 5 plus the CHECKs in step 8.
  SELECT t.{{ s.state_column }}, t.head_seq, t.{{ s.epoch_column }}{% if binding.emit_outbox %}, t.site_id{% endif %}

    INTO v_state, v_head, v_epoch{% if binding.emit_outbox %}, v_site_id{% endif %}

    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} t
   WHERE t.{{ s.id_column }} = a_subject_id
     FOR UPDATE;
  IF v_state IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE = 'P0001',
      MESSAGE = '{{ binding.name }}: merge refused by {{ binding.schema }}.merge_{{ s.kind }}'
                || ' — no {{ s.kind }} with that identifier';
  END IF;

  -- ── 2 · RE-DERIVE UNDER A SHARED LOCK ────────────────────────────────────────
  -- This is the anti-join of §6.5. It takes shared locks on the obligation rows so a
  -- concurrent materialisation conflicts here rather than after the CHECKs have
  -- passed (anomaly A1), and it produces the number the event payload records.
  --
  -- IT DOES NOT DECIDE. The refusal for a non-zero result belongs to
  -- {{ counter.constraint }} when the counter agrees, and to
  -- {{ binding.schema }}.fn_{{ stem }}_merge_gate when it does not — conformance cases CF-01,
  -- CF-02 and CF-03 name those two exhibits, and a refusal raised HERE would carry
  -- the name of this procedure instead, which is the wrong diagnosis for the right
  -- outcome.
  SELECT count(*) INTO v_open
    FROM (SELECT 1
            FROM {{ binding.schema }}.blocking_check bc
           WHERE bc.{{ s.id_column }} = a_subject_id
             AND NOT EXISTS (
                   SELECT 1 FROM {{ binding.schema }}.disposition d
                    WHERE d.check_id = bc.check_id
                      AND d.retracted_by IS NULL
                      AND (d.expires_at IS NULL OR d.expires_at > now()))
             FOR SHARE) AS locked;

  -- ── 3 · PROJECT THE PREDECESSOR CHAIN DIGEST ─────────────────────────────────
  -- The chain link is READ from the predecessor rather than accepted from the caller.
  -- `fn_{{ stem }}_event_chain` verifies it on insert either way, so on this path the
  -- verification is tautological and on every OTHER writer's path it is the whole
  -- defence — which is the point of enforcing at the table rather than at the caller.
  -- A missing predecessor leaves this NULL, the genesis value goes in, and that
  -- trigger refuses with `P0001`: the gate does not invent a chain.
  SELECT e.chain_digest INTO v_prev_digest
    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.{{ stem }}_event e
   WHERE e.{{ s.id_column }} = a_subject_id AND e.seq = v_head;

  -- ── 4 · PROJECT THE CLEARANCE DIGEST ─────────────────────────────────────────
  -- SHA-256 over the sorted (check_id, disposition_id) set: exactly which obligations
  -- were cleared, and by which signatures, at the instant of the merge. Computed by
  -- the SERVER from the base tables, so the completion record cannot claim a
  -- clearance set the database does not hold. A subject with no obligations digests
  -- the empty string, which is a stable, checkable value rather than a NULL.
  SELECT digest(coalesce(string_agg(bc.check_id::STRING || ':' || d.disposition_id::STRING, '|'
                                    ORDER BY bc.check_id::STRING, d.disposition_id::STRING), ''),
                'sha256')
    INTO v_clearance
    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.blocking_check bc
    JOIN {{ binding.schema }}.disposition d
      ON d.check_id = bc.check_id AND d.retracted_by IS NULL
   WHERE bc.{{ s.id_column }} = a_subject_id;

  -- ── 5 · APPEND THE TRANSITION EVENT ──────────────────────────────────────────
  -- `23505` on the CAS means someone moved the head; `23503` on `legal_edge` means
  -- the edge {{ s.state_column }} → '{{ s.completing_state }}' is not in the transition table (CF-13).
  -- `from_state` is the state actually read in step 1, never an assumed one, so an
  -- illegal transition is refused by data rather than by an assumption.
  --
  -- The payload carries what the gate OBSERVED, appended server-side to what the
  -- caller supplied. It is inside `chain_digest`, so the observation is chained.
  INSERT INTO {{ binding.schema }}.{{ stem }}_event
              ({{ s.id_column }}, seq, prev_seq, from_state, to_state, subject_kind,
               actor_sub, payload, prev_digest)
       VALUES (a_subject_id, v_head + 1, v_head, v_state, '{{ s.completing_state }}', '{{ s.kind }}',
               a_merged_by,
               a_payload || jsonb_build_object('gate_observed_open', v_open,
                                               '{{ s.epoch_column }}', v_epoch),
               coalesce(v_prev_digest, {{ genesis }}));

  -- ── 6 · PIN THE EPOCH ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- The composite foreign key ({{ s.id_column }}, {{ s.epoch_column }}) → {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} under
  -- ON UPDATE RESTRICT is what makes attaching a later obligation to a completed
  -- transition PHYSICALLY IMPOSSIBLE: every new obligation bumps {{ s.epoch_column }}, and the
  -- bump is refused while this row references the old value (MI07, anomaly A2).
  -- `merge_record_pkey` refuses a second merge of the same subject (CF-09, MI09).
  INSERT INTO {{ binding.schema }}.merge_record
              (subject_kind, subject_id, {{ s.id_column }}, {{ s.epoch_column }},
               merged_by, merged_commit, clearance_digest)
       VALUES ('{{ s.kind }}', a_subject_id, a_subject_id, v_epoch,
               a_merged_by, a_merged_commit, v_clearance);
{% if binding.emit_outbox %}

  -- ── 7 · THE CUSTODY LEDGER, IN THE SAME TRANSACTION (INV-3) ──────────────────
  -- Intake only. Sequencing is an anti-join performed by the sequencer, so the entire
  -- ledger write path stays INSERT + SELECT and a merge never waits on it.
  -- `site_code` is PROJECTED from the site table rather than supplied: the ledger
  -- partition key is a fact about the subject, not a claim by the merging client, and
  -- an unknown site refuses (rule P-2).
  SELECT st.site_code INTO v_site_code
    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.site st WHERE st.site_id = v_site_id;
  IF v_site_code IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE = 'P0001',
      MESSAGE = '{{ binding.name }}: merge refused by {{ binding.schema }}.merge_{{ s.kind }}'
                || ' — no site record for this {{ s.kind }}';
  END IF;
  INSERT INTO {{ binding.schema }}.ledger_intake
              (site_code, entry_kind, subject_id, actor, actor_kind,
               payload, canon_bytes, payload_ver, leaf_hash, hlc)
       VALUES (v_site_code, 'merge', a_subject_id, a_merged_by, a_actor_kind,
               a_payload, a_canon_bytes, a_payload_ver, a_leaf_hash,
               -- UNQUALIFIED, and measured. `crdb_internal.cluster_logical_timestamp()`
               -- — the form ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 uses — does not resolve on v26.2.5
               -- (`42883: unknown function`). The builtin lives in the global scope.
               cluster_logical_timestamp());
{% else %}

  -- ── 7 · THE CUSTODY LEDGER — NOT RENDERED FOR THIS BINDING ───────────────────
  -- This arm writes {{ binding.schema }}.ledger_intake and reads {{ binding.schema }}.site, neither of
  -- which this binding declares or owns (`emit_outbox = false`, ruling D9). Procedures
  -- bind EARLY on v26.2, so naming an absent relation would make this migration
  -- un-appliable rather than degrade it; the switch is therefore at render time and
  -- this comment is the committed fallback a reviewer reads (ruling D5).
  --
  -- `a_actor_kind`, `a_canon_bytes`, `a_payload_ver` and `a_leaf_hash` are accepted
  -- and unused here ON PURPOSE. One ABI across every binding is worth more than four
  -- saved parameters: `trappoint_core.gate.execute_gate` issues one `CALL` shape, and
  -- a signature that varied by binding would put vertical knowledge into the client.
{% endif %}

  -- ── 8 · THE LAST WRITE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- Every CHECK on {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }} fires HERE, on the completing row, and
  -- `fn_{{ stem }}_merge_gate` runs immediately before them. `head_seq = v_head` is the
  -- compare-and-swap; `RETURNING … INTO` is how a zero-row UPDATE is detected, because
  -- `GET DIAGNOSTICS … ROW_COUNT` is unimplemented on this platform. A procedure that
  -- committed here having matched no row would leave a merge_record for a merge that
  -- never happened, which is why this is one of the only two refusals in the file.
  UPDATE {{ binding.schema }}.{{ s.table }}
     SET {{ s.state_column }} = '{{ s.completing_state }}',
         head_seq = v_head + 1,
         merged_commit = a_merged_commit
   WHERE {{ s.id_column }} = a_subject_id AND head_seq = v_head
  RETURNING head_seq INTO v_new_head;
  IF v_new_head IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION USING ERRCODE = 'P0001',
      MESSAGE = '{{ binding.name }}: merge refused by {{ binding.schema }}.merge_{{ s.kind }}'
                || ' — the head moved under the gate transaction';
  END IF;
END $proc$;
{% endfor %}
{% if binding.emit_outbox %}
-- @file 0119_fn_ledger_cas_append.sql
{{ header(file='0119_fn_ledger_cas_append.sql',
          title='CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION ' ~ binding.schema ~ '.fn_ledger_cas_append() — gap-free by compare-and-swap, so a gap MEANS tampering',
          rationale='A sequence would make a gap ambiguous: crash, rollback, cache loss and tampering would all look alike, and the ledger asserts nothing it cannot distinguish. Deriving seq inside the serializable transaction as coalesce(max(seq)+1, 0) makes the primary key itself the compare-and-swap, so two writers racing for one position collide on 23505 and the surviving sequence is dense by construction rather than by convention.',
          mi=['MI24'],
          i=['I04'],
          counsel_gated=false) }}
--
-- THE BAN IS THE POINT. `CREATE SEQUENCE`, `nextval(`, `SERIAL` and `unique_rowid()`
-- are refused by `trappoint render` and by `trappoint migrate lint` (ruling D10), and
-- that lint is LOAD-BEARING rather than decorative: ground-truth finding F4 measured
-- that `CREATE SEQUENCE` succeeds on this cluster. One future migration reintroducing
-- one would make every gap in the ledger deniable, and deniability is the only thing
-- the custody chain exists to remove.
--
-- TWO COMPARE-AND-SWAPS, NOT ONE. `ledger_leaf_pkey (site_code, seq)` refuses a
-- duplicate position; `ledger_linear (site_code, prev_link_hash)` refuses a fork at
-- the same predecessor. Either alone would leave the other anomaly representable, and
-- both are enforced against every writer, including one holding a psql session.
--
-- `prev_link_hash` IS NEVER NULL. Genesis is 32 zero bytes (custody CU-1), so the
-- link function is total and a verifier never branches on NULL.
--
-- SEQUENCED-NESS IS DERIVED, NEVER WRITTEN: there is no `sequenced` flag on
-- `ledger_intake` and this function does not write one. The sequencer's batch is an
-- anti-join, which is what keeps the whole ledger path INSERT + SELECT.
--
-- RETURNS the position it took, so a caller can build the Signed Disposition Receipt
-- without a second round trip.

CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION {{ binding.schema }}.fn_ledger_cas_append(
  a_site_code STRING,
  a_entry_id  UUID,
  a_leaf_hash BYTES,
  a_batch_id  UUID
) RETURNS INT8 LANGUAGE PLpgSQL AS $fn$
DECLARE
  v_seq  INT8;
  v_prev BYTES;
BEGIN
  -- Derived INSIDE the caller's serializable transaction. Two appenders that read the
  -- same max() both try to write the same position, and the primary key decides.
  SELECT coalesce(max(l.seq) + 1, 0) INTO v_seq
    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.ledger_leaf l
   WHERE l.site_code = a_site_code;

  SELECT l.link_hash INTO v_prev
    FROM {{ binding.schema }}.ledger_leaf l
   WHERE l.site_code = a_site_code AND l.seq = v_seq - 1;

  INSERT INTO {{ binding.schema }}.ledger_leaf
              (site_code, seq, entry_id, leaf_hash, prev_link_hash, link_hash, batch_id)
       VALUES (a_site_code, v_seq, a_entry_id, a_leaf_hash,
               coalesce(v_prev, {{ genesis }}),
               digest(coalesce(v_prev, {{ genesis }}) || a_leaf_hash, 'sha256'),
               a_batch_id);
  RETURN v_seq;
END $fn$;
{% endif %}
